WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:08.735
"You say that I've been kind to you?" The earl repeated his question to Cedric in the castle library.

2
00:00:08.736 --> 00:00:13.306
Dougal, the earl's dog, sat next to Cedric's chair.

3
00:00:13.307 --> 00:00:21.075
"Oh, yes! I'm forever grateful to you for helping the apple seller and Dick and Mary."

4
00:00:21.076 --> 00:00:30.316
"The apple seller!" the earl exclaimed. "Dick! Mary! Who are these people?"

5
00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:35.821
"I forgot that you've never met them!" Cedric laughed.

6
00:00:35.822 --> 00:00:38.538
"They're special friends of mine.

7
00:00:38.539 --> 00:00:42.417
I helped them with the money that you gave to Mr. Havisham.

8
00:00:42.418 --> 00:00:46.406
You said I could spend it any way I liked."

9
00:00:46.407 --> 00:00:50.834
Cedric explained what he'd done for his friends.

10
00:00:50.835 --> 00:00:57.928
While Cedric talked, the earl quietly observed the dog.

11
00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:03.753
"Dougal usually doesn't make friends quickly," the earl thought.

12
00:01:03.754 --> 00:01:08.166
"But he liked this young fellow right away."

13
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:12.579
At that moment the big dog put his head on the boy's knee.

14
00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.071
Cedric petted him while continuing to talk.

15
00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:22.890
"You'd like Dick," Cedric said. "He does everything fair and square."

16
00:01:22.891 --> 00:01:27.156
"What on earth does that mean?"

17
00:01:27.157 --> 00:01:33.696
"Well . . ." Cedric wasn't quite sure, but he knew it was something positive.

18
00:01:33.697 --> 00:01:40.067
"I think it means he wouldn't cheat anyone," Cedric said slowly.

19
00:01:40.068 --> 00:01:44.098
He sat forward in his chair as he grew more confident.

20
00:01:44.099 --> 00:01:48.958
"That's right. Or hit a boy who was smaller than he was.

21
00:01:48.959 --> 00:01:52.530
He's really dedicated to his work too.

22
00:01:52.531 --> 00:01:57.927
He makes sure his customers' shoes are as shiny as possible."

23
00:01:57.928 --> 00:02:02.641
Cedric reached into his pocket and pulled out Dick's handkerchief.

24
00:02:02.642 --> 00:02:09.360
He regarded it with a mix of affection and pride before handing it to the earl.

25
00:02:09.361 --> 00:02:14.344
"Dick gave me a present before the ship sailed."

26
00:02:14.345 --> 00:02:21.253
"Um . . . it's very bright." The earl quickly handed back the handkerchief.

27
00:02:21.254 --> 00:02:24.930
"I'm going to keep it forever."

28
00:02:24.931 --> 00:02:29.894
Cedric admired the handkerchief again before returning it to his pocket.

29
00:02:29.895 --> 00:02:37.111
"And this Dick is one of your acquaintances, is he?" the earl asked.

30
00:02:37.112 --> 00:02:44.401
"He's an old friend. Well, not quite as old as Mr. Hobbs," Cedric said.

31
00:02:44.402 --> 00:02:50.728
"Oh! I haven't told you about Mr. Hobbs the grocer—my best friend."

32
00:02:50.729 --> 00:02:55.034
The earl couldn't imagine what Cedric would say next.

33
00:02:55.035 --> 00:03:03.406
He was still trying to grasp that his grandson's friends were a shoeshine boy, an apple seller, and a servant.

34
00:03:03.407 --> 00:03:09.319
Cedric continued talking about the grocer. "I bought Mr. Hobbs a gold watch.

35
00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.188
That's something else I did with your money. I

36
00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:19.835
had it inscribed with some poetry: When this you see, remember me. "

37
00:03:19.836 --> 00:03:29.932
Cedric sighed. "I'll always remember Mr. Hobbs—and my other friends too."

38
00:03:29.933 --> 00:03:34.014
In his travels, the earl had seen a great deal of the world.

39
00:03:34.015 --> 00:03:40.162
But here in his own home was something so unusual that it took his breath away.

40
00:03:40.163 --> 00:03:44.677
The earl had never liked children, not even his own.

41
00:03:44.678 --> 00:03:51.560
But this boy—this American boy—was actually entertaining.

42
00:03:51.561 --> 00:03:56.371
The door to the library opened, and a footman entered.

43
00:03:56.372 --> 00:04:00.588
"Dinner is served, my lord," he said.

44
00:04:00.589 --> 00:04:06.141
Cedric stood up and studied the earl's gouty foot.

45
00:04:06.142 --> 00:04:09.937
"Would you like me to help you?" Cedric said.

46
00:04:09.938 --> 00:04:13.117
"You could lean on me when you walk.

47
00:04:13.118 --> 00:04:18.012
One time a potato barrel rolled over Mr. Hobbs' foot.

48
00:04:18.013 --> 00:04:23.190
Afterward his foot hurt, so he leaned on me."

49
00:04:23.191 --> 00:04:27.108
The earl looked his grandson up and down.

50
00:04:27.109 --> 00:04:31.155
"Are you sure you could support me?"

51
00:04:31.156 --> 00:04:34.456
"I think I could," Cedric said.

52
00:04:34.457 --> 00:04:39.287
"I'm pretty strong, and you could use your cane on the other side.

53
00:04:39.288 --> 00:04:43.316
Dick said I have a lot of muscle for a nine-year-old."

54
00:04:43.317 --> 00:04:49.045
Cedric held up both arms and flexed his muscles for the earl.

55
00:04:49.046 --> 00:04:55.341
"All right, you may try," his grandfather replied.

56
00:04:55.342 --> 00:04:59.599
Cedric handed the earl his cane and helped him to stand.

57
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.651
Usually this was the footman's job.

58
00:05:03.652 --> 00:05:10.101
If the earl felt any pain in his foot, he relieved it by shouting at the footman.

59
00:05:10.102 --> 00:05:14.698
But this time he didn't shout—even when his foot hurt.

60
00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:22.914
"Don't be afraid to lean on my shoulder," Cedric said with an encouraging smile. "I'll walk slowly."

61
00:05:22.915 --> 00:05:26.908
The earl didn't hesitate to rest his weight on Cedric.

62
00:05:26.909 --> 00:05:32.399
He wanted to see how much strength the boy possessed.

63
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.642
When they entered the dining room, Cedric's heart sank.

64
00:05:36.643 --> 00:05:45.735
He was already tired. It was a long way to the earl's chair at the head of the table.

65
00:05:45.736 --> 00:05:52.305
By the time he'd helped the earl to sit, Cedric's face was red and he was panting.

66
00:05:52.306 --> 00:05:58.030
"Perhaps the heat from the fireplace helps your foot."

67
00:05:58.031 --> 00:06:01.526
Cedric wiped his forehead with Dick's handkerchief.

68
00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:05.501
"But it seems a bit warm to me."

69
00:06:05.502 --> 00:06:10.715
The earl noted Cedric's courage and his respectful attitude.

70
00:06:10.716 --> 00:06:16.196
"You've been doing some hard work," the earl said.

71
00:06:16.197 --> 00:06:18.912
Cedric wiped his face again.

72
00:06:18.913 --> 00:06:26.719
"It wasn't too hard, but a person does get warm in the summer."

